    section: "C", marks: 3, format: "Short", difficulty: "Easy", bloomSkill: "Applying",
    questionText: "You have a piece of construction paper that measures 32 cm by 48 cm. You want to cut it into squares of equal size.\n(i) What will be the dimensions of the largest possible square?\n(ii) How many such squares will you have?",
    solutionSteps: ["[1 mark] The side of the largest square = HCF(32, 48). By prime factorization: 32 = 2^5 and 48 = 2^4 x 3.", "[1 mark] HCF = product of the lowest powers of common prime factors = 2^4 = 16. So the largest square is 16 cm by 16 cm.", "[1 mark] Number of squares = (32/16) x (48/16) = 2 x 3 = 6 squares."],
