    questionText: "Flipkart sells items packed into various sizes of cartons. The inner dimensions (Length x Width) in cm are: Small 6 x 8, Medium 12 x 24, Large 24 x 36, Extra large 36 x 48, XXL 48 x 96. Flipkart places supporting thermocol sheets inside every package along the edges, and wants the same sized sheet for all carton types.\n(i) What should be the maximum size of the sheet that fits into all types of cartons?\n(ii) How many such sheet sizes are possible?\n(iii) A new carton called semi large measures 14 x 15. Does the existing maximum size sheet fit it?\n(iv) What should the semi large carton size have been (larger than medium but smaller than large) so that the maximum sheet stays the same?",
    solutionSteps: ["[1 mark] Maximum sheet length = HCF of the lengths (6, 12, 24, 36, 48). By prime factorization the common factor is 2 x 3 = 6, so HCF = 6. Maximum sheet width = HCF of the widths (8, 24, 36, 48, 96) = 4. So the maximum sheet is 6 cm by 4 cm.", "[1 mark] HCF is unique, so only ONE such sheet size is possible.", "[1 mark] For the 14 x 15 carton: 14 is not a multiple of 6 and 15 is not a multiple of 4, so the 6 x 4 sheet does NOT fit.", "[1 mark] To keep the maximum sheet at 6 x 4, the new size must have length a multiple of 6 and width a multiple of 4, between medium (12 x 24) and large (24 x 36): e.g. 18 x 28 or 18 x 32."],
