    questionText: "A jeweller has two bars of gold alloy in stock, one of 12 carats and the other of 18 carats (24-carat gold is pure gold, so 12-carat is 12/24 pure and 18-carat is 18/24 pure). How many grams of each alloy must be mixed to obtain 10 grams of 14-carat gold?",
    solutionSteps: ["[1 mark] Let x = grams of 12-carat alloy, y = grams of 18-carat alloy. Total mass: x + y = 10.", "[1 mark] Pure-gold balance: (12/24)x + (18/24)y = (14/24) * 10. Multiply through by 24: 12x + 18y = 140.", "[1 mark] From x + y = 10 we get x = 10 - y. Substitute: 12(10 - y) + 18y = 140 => 120 + 6y = 140 => 6y = 20.", "[1 mark] y = 20/6 = 10/3 grams (about 3.33 g) of 18-carat gold.", "[1 mark] x = 10 - 10/3 = 20/3 grams (about 6.67 g) of 12-carat gold. Check: (12/24)(20/3) + (18/24)(10/3) = 10/3 + 30/12... = 35/6 = (14/24)(10), correct."],
